import json
from pathlib import Path
rows=[]
def add(n,title,question,prediction,concept,defense,verification,reflection,answers):
 rows.append(dict(id=n,title=title,question=question,prediction=prediction,concept=concept,defense=defense,verification=verification,reflection=reflection,answers=answers))
add(1,'Who Are You?','How do users and processes carry identity?',
 'An ordinary program will inherit the invoking user\'s real and effective IDs. Running as Alice will change those credentials.',
 'Real IDs identify the originating user; effective IDs normally drive discretionary access checks. The filesystem UID can matter for Linux file checks. Authentication establishes identity; authorization evaluates a requested operation.',
 'Use separate unprivileged accounts and grant only required access.',
 'Compare id with identity.c under the student and Alice accounts.',
 'A compromised process can exercise its credentials; shared accounts weaken attribution.',
 {'1':'UID records the real user identity; EUID is the effective identity used for many access decisions. They can differ with setuid or deliberate credential changes. No setuid program is created here.',
 '2':'Authentication proves an identity (for example, possession of an SSH key). Authorization decides whether that identity may read a file or perform another action.',
 '3':'Unauthorized reading violates confidentiality. Network listeners and parsers handling untrusted files are two workstation attack surfaces.'})
add(2,'Meet the Kernel','Where does an application request privileged resource operations?',
 'The file operation will appear as openat/open, write and close system calls; startup adds loader calls.',
 'A system call transfers control from user space into the kernel, which validates arguments and mediates resources. The kernel is part of the trusted computing base (TCB).',
 'Keep applications unprivileged and rely on validated kernel interfaces.',
 'The trace contains the hello.txt open, message write and descriptor close; verify file contents.',
 'A userspace library call is not always a one-to-one syscall. Tracing can change timing.',
 {'4':'Consult the captured trace: Linux libc commonly implements open using openat. write sends bytes and close releases the descriptor.',
 '5':'Direct unrestricted hardware access would bypass memory isolation, device arbitration and permission checks, allowing one application to damage other workloads.',
 '6':'The kernel enforces process isolation and access policy; if it lies or is compromised, userspace security assumptions fail.'})
add(3,'Process Detective','What security metadata does /proc expose?',
 'A sleeping process will expose credentials, one thread, memory counters, descriptor entries and process start information.',
 '/proc presents kernel-maintained process state. CapEff, NoNewPrivs, Seccomp and TracerPid add security context beyond the starter script.',
 'Investigate unexpected credentials, tracing, capabilities, descriptor access and resource use.',
 'Run the inspector on a live process we own; reject invalid PID strings and count actual fd entries.',
 'A process can exit or a PID can be reused during collection. Permission errors are not evidence of zero descriptors.',
 {'7':'Status exposes UIDs/GIDs, parent PID, state, threads, memory, capabilities, seccomp and no-new-privileges. Some information is restricted by procfs policy.',
 '8':'Excess CPU, memory, threads or descriptors can exhaust shared resources and threaten availability; cgroups and limits constrain impact.',
 '9':'Unexpected thread counts can reveal concurrency, runaway workers or resource abuse, but a high count alone is not proof of malicious behavior.'})
add(4,'Can One Process Read Another?','Does a pointer identify memory across processes?',
 'The address belongs to one virtual address space. Maps will show executable, libraries, stack and anonymous regions; ASLR may vary addresses between launches.',
 'Page tables and MMU permissions translate process virtual addresses to physical memory. The same numeric address may refer to different pages in another process.',
 'Use process isolation, least privilege and mediated debugging interfaces.',
 'Capture maps while the process is alive and compare two independent launches.',
 'Ordinary pointer dereferencing cannot cross an address-space boundary. ptrace, process_vm_readv and /proc/PID/mem are controlled exceptions, not impossible mechanisms.',
 {'10':'An address space contains executable code, data, heap, stack, mapped files/libraries, anonymous mappings and kernel-provided helper mappings where applicable.',
 '11':'A pointer is interpreted through the current process\'s page tables. Another process cannot use it to directly select the first process\'s pages.',
 '12':'The MMU enforces per-page mappings and read/write/execute permissions configured by the kernel, producing a fault on disallowed access.'})
add(5,'The Race','Can concurrent increments lose updates?',
 'Several unsynchronized runs may fall below two million; the mutex runs should always reach two million.',
 'Increment is a read-modify-write critical section. Concurrent non-atomic accesses constitute a C data race and undefined behavior. -O0 aids observation but does not make the race valid or deterministic.',
 'Protect every access to shared state with the same mutex, or use suitable C atomics.',
 'Repeat five unsafe runs and three mutex runs; separately run five exact-count regression checks.',
 'A race could bypass a quota, double-spend a balance or invalidate a check/use assumption. A correct count in one unsafe run does not prove safety.',
 {'13':'Two threads can read the same old value and both store the same incremented value, losing an update. More broadly, the C program has undefined behavior.',
 '14':'counter++ is the critical section. The protected version acquires lock before it and releases lock afterward.',
 '15':'A check and update can interleave so that an authorization, balance or resource limit is enforced inconsistently.'})
add(6,'Two Processes Need to Talk','How do pipe and shared-memory IPC differ?',
 'Both demos will transmit a fixed parent message. The pipe serializes bytes through the kernel; a process-shared semaphore orders access to a shared mapping.',
 'A pipe is a kernel-managed byte stream accessed through inherited descriptors. Shared memory exposes common pages; synchronization establishes publication and visibility.',
 'Limit descriptor inheritance, close unused pipe ends, bound messages, and protect shared-memory permissions and synchronization.',
 'Check exact pipe text and shared-memory receipt after semaphore publication.',
 'The supplied handout refers to instructor shared-memory code that was absent. shared_ipc.c is a self-contained comparison using an anonymous shared mapping inherited by fork.',
 {'16':'A pipe uses read/write operations and kernel buffering; shared memory allows direct loads/stores into shared pages after setup.',
 '17':'Without synchronization, concurrent readers and writers can observe partial or inconsistent state. Semaphore ordering prevents reading before publication.',
 '18':'Protect both endpoints and data: unauthorized endpoint access enables injection or disclosure even when message formatting is correct.'})
add(7,'Alice vs Bob','How does chmod change a second user\'s access?',
 'Bob can read an Alice-owned 0644 file and is denied after it becomes 0600; Alice retains read access.',
 'DAC uses ownership, group membership and mode bits. The owner can change the policy, subject to filesystem and other controls.',
 'Restrict secret files to the smallest needed audience.',
 'Capture stat before/after and assert both denied Bob access and allowed Alice access.',
 'File modes do not help if an ancestor directory or another copy exposes the same data.',
 {'19':'0644 grants others read access; 0600 removes group and other permissions, leaving the owner read/write.',
 '20':'The file owner normally controls its discretionary mode and ACL, while privileged administrators may override ordinary DAC.',
 '21':'Removing Bob\'s unnecessary access illustrates least privilege. Owner, group and other are three distinct permission classes; each has r/w/x bits.'})
add(8,'The Permission Maze','Can one named user receive a narrow exception?',
 'A named Bob read ACL will permit reading without granting write or making the file world-readable.',
 'POSIX ACLs supplement owner/group/other with named entries. The ACL mask bounds effective permissions of named users and group-class entries.',
 'Grant u:bob:r, verify effective rights, then remove the temporary exception.',
 'Capture getfacl before and after, test read allowed/write denied, remove ACL and prove read denied again.',
 'ls mode bits alone can obscure ACL intent: the group-class bits reflect the ACL mask, not simply the owning group entry.',
 {'22':'The named Bob entry grants read access subject to the mask; the owner remains Alice.',
 '23':'An ACL can identify Bob individually without granting the same permission to every member of the owning group or all other users.',
 '24':'Fine-grained least privilege is exercised. ACLs are still discretionary access controls and do not inherently stop an owner from changing policy.'})
add(9,'Too Much Power','How can Linux express privileges more narrowly than root?',
 'An ordinary shell will have few or no effective capabilities. System file capabilities depend on the installed image.',
 'Linux capabilities divide privileged operations into sets associated with threads and executable files. They are not identical to unforgeable object-capability systems.',
 'Separate privileged setup from unprivileged processing; drop capability sets and avoid unnecessary file capabilities.',
 'Capture id, sudo policy, capsh and existing getcap output without granting new privileges.',
 'Some capabilities, especially CAP_SYS_ADMIN, remain very broad; a capability grant needs individual analysis.',
 {'25':'Root can bypass many discretionary checks and administer critical resources, so compromise has a much larger blast radius.',
 '26':'Linux capabilities split traditional root authority into named privileges such as CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE. They are per-thread kernel privileges, not general-purpose object handles.',
 '27':'Keep the network/parser component unprivileged and isolate a small audited helper for the one privileged operation it needs.'})
add(10,'The Dangerous Array','What happens when a fixed input exceeds a stack buffer?',
 'Short input fits; the protected or ASan build should stop the long copy. The bounded implementation rejects input longer than 15 bytes.',
 'A 16-byte array needs one byte for the string terminator. Writing 34 bytes crosses its boundary and causes undefined behavior. No exploit payload or foreign process is used.',
 'Reject oversize inputs before copying; retain stack protection and sanitizers during testing.',
 'Capture compilation, short success, protected long-input nonzero exit, ASan stack-buffer-overflow and exact safe boundaries.',
 'An unprotected build is inspected but not exploited. A crash is one possible symptom; it is not a guaranteed outcome of undefined behavior.',
 {'28':'It means accessing bytes outside the array\'s allocated object; adjacent state can be overwritten and C provides no valid semantics for the operation.',
 '29':'Corruption can damage data, crash a process or alter control flow. This lab only observes local detection and termination.',
 '30':'A pre-copy size check includes the terminator and rejects an input that will not fit, preventing the out-of-bounds operation rather than detecting it afterward.'})
add(11,'Why Did the Defense Work?','Which layers protect a modern executable?',
 'The protected binary should show PIE, a canary, non-executable stack and full RELRO. The comparison disables canary and PIE while retaining NX.',
 'ASLR randomizes mappings; PIE permits executable relocation. NX removes execute permission from data pages. Canaries check for certain stack overwrites. RELRO protects relocation metadata.',
 'Keep compiler hardening enabled and repair unsafe memory operations; mitigations do not establish memory safety.',
 'Capture checksec for both builds, randomize_va_space, GNU_STACK flags and __stack_chk_fail symbol.',
 'A canary detects some overwrites on function return, not every memory error. ASLR adds uncertainty rather than eliminating corruption.',
 {'31':'ASLR makes mapping locations such as stack, libraries and PIE text less predictable across launches.',
 '32':'NX prevents instruction execution on pages lacking execute permission; it does not prevent data corruption or every code-reuse technique.',
 '33':'A stack canary is a guard checked before returning from protected functions to detect some stack corruption.',
 '34':'Independent controls cover different failure modes, so one bypass need not defeat every protection. Bounds checks remain the primary fix.'})
add(12,'The Secret File','How do file and directory permissions combine?',
 'Alice, as a securelab group member, can traverse secrets and read incident.txt. Bob cannot traverse it. Alice cannot overwrite the root-owned record.',
 'Directory execute controls traversal; read controls name listing; write plus execute controls entry changes. A file permission alone is insufficient.',
 'Use root:securelab, 0750 restricted directories and 0640 records; keep public data separate.',
 'Capture namei path components; test Alice read allowed, Bob read denied and Alice write denied.',
 'World-readable content can still be hidden by a non-traversable directory, but relying on that alone is fragile when files are moved or copied.',
 {'35':'Each ancestor must allow traversal before a file can be opened; directory write/execute can also permit replacement or deletion even when the file itself is not writable.',
 '36':'Root and explicitly authorized securelab group members such as Alice; Bob has no business need.',
 '37':'If reachable through traversable directories, a world-readable incident file could disclose confidential data. Use restrictive file and directory permissions together.'})
add(13,'Can We Trust the File?','Can a checksum detect modification?',
 'The baseline check will pass, then fail after appending text.',
 'SHA-256 fingerprints bytes. Comparison with a trusted baseline detects drift; an unauthenticated checksum cannot identify the author or resist replacement of both data and baseline.',
 'Protect the baseline separately; use signatures or HMAC when authenticity is needed.',
 'Record both hashes and assert sha256sum -c changes from success to failure.',
 'Hashing is not secure deletion. Unlink removes a name, and overwriting is unreliable on SSDs, snapshots and copy-on-write storage. Encryption-key management and platform-appropriate sanitization matter.',
 {'38':'Changing file bytes produces a different SHA-256 digest with overwhelming probability; consult the captured original and modified digests.',
 '39':'No. Anyone can hash bytes. A hash alone neither proves authorship nor protects an attacker-writable baseline.',
 '40':'Integrity asks whether bytes match a trusted reference; authenticity asks whether their claimed origin is trustworthy.'})
add(14,'What Happened While I Was Away?','Can logs reconstruct an event sequence?',
 'Eight harmless explicitly labeled synthetic events will appear in timestamp order under a run-specific journal tag.',
 'journald associates messages with timestamps and origin metadata. Logging is evidence subject to coverage, clock and retention limits.',
 'Keep useful persistent logs, restrict access, synchronize time and consider remote tamper-resistant collection.',
 'Synchronize the journal and require all eight tagged events; collect SSH and login context separately.',
 'The eight messages are deliberate lab markers, not automatic proof that their described security actions happened. Actual action evidence is in the corresponding mission logs.',
 {'41':'Authentication failures/successes, service changes, policy denials and privileged actions are useful, if the relevant subsystem actually logs them.',
 '42':'Timestamps order events and correlate sources; clock skew, timezone conversion and coarse precision must be considered.',
 '43':'Unlogged actions, lost/rotated records, attacker tampering and misleading application messages limit conclusions. Absence of a log is not proof of absence.'})
add(15,'The Suspicious Application','Which resources does a harmless program need?',
 'address_space needs its executable/libraries, a stack and stdout, but no listening socket or secret files.',
 'Attack surface is the set of reachable interfaces and resources that can be misused. A resource inventory motivates confinement.',
 'Restrict network access, sensitive file access and writable filesystem areas; bound memory and process creation.',
 'Inspect our live process\'s credentials, descriptors and sockets; distinguish all-system ss output from per-process lsof output.',
 'Observing no access during a short run does not prove the program cannot access a resource. A policy needs explicit enforcement and regression checks.',
 {'44':'The address-space demo needs executable/library mappings, ordinary memory, timing/sleep syscalls and stdout. It has no application need for network or secret-file access.',
 '45':'Deny network creation, home/secret reads and arbitrary filesystem writes; cap processes and memory. Policy tests must still confirm normal operation.',
 '46':'Least privilege reduces what a compromised component can read, modify or invoke. Mission 17 demonstrates file confinement and Mission 19 applies service confinement.'})
add(16,'Build Your First Container Sandbox','Which boundaries does a container actually provide?',
 'The container shares the guest kernel but has a distinct PID namespace; cgroup files will expose configured memory, CPU and PID limits.',
 'Namespaces isolate resource views; cgroups constrain accounting and consumption. Containers are processes under the same kernel, not separate guest kernels.',
 'Use no network, read-only root filesystem, no added capabilities, no-new-privileges and resource limits. No ports are published.',
 'Capture hello-world and Ubuntu output, namespace inode, cgroup limits and rejected root-filesystem write.',
 'Docker daemon access is highly privileged. It is installed only in the disposable guest and used through sudo, without granting docker-group membership.',
 {'47':'A PID namespace gives the container a process-ID view in which its first process can be PID 1. Host/guest parent namespaces may still see those processes.',
 '48':'A VM has virtual hardware and its own guest kernel. A container shares its host kernel; here that host is the Ubuntu VM.',
 '49':'Cgroups can control memory, CPU bandwidth, process counts and device/I/O access depending on controllers and configuration. Namespaces alone do not impose resource quotas.'})
add(17,'Linux Says No','Can MAC deny a file that DAC allows?',
 'Both world-readable teaching files will open unconfined. The enforcing AppArmor profile will allow one and deny the other.',
 'AppArmor attaches a mandatory policy to a program and mediates operations independently of owner-controlled DAC grants. Effective access must satisfy both layers.',
 'Keep the profile enforcing, grant specific required paths and audit the explicit denial.',
 'First prove DAC permits both, load the profile, then assert allowed.txt opens and denied.txt does not; collect profile status and available kernel denial logs.',
 'The program return code is the main behavior check. Journal availability and policy logging can vary; a missing denial log does not by itself mean access was allowed.',
 {'50':'AppArmor mediates configured operations of a confined task, including filesystem access and optionally other resources; it is not simply a file owner setting.',
 '51':'DAC owners can grant ordinary file access; a mandatory profile can still deny the program even when its UID and file mode would permit the operation.',
 '52':'A compromised application that satisfies ordinary DAC can still be blocked from unrelated resources by MAC. The profile and kernel are trusted dependencies.'})
add(18,'Compare Access-Control Models','Who decides access under each model?',
 'The comparison will connect observed DAC, ACL, capability and MAC behavior; RBAC remains a conceptual scenario.',
 'Different models answer different questions: identity and ownership, mandatory policy, role membership, named ACL rights or privileged kernel operations.',
 'Combine models deliberately and verify effective access instead of assuming one mechanism subsumes all others.',
 'Re-test Alice/Bob and the enforcing AppArmor profile; consult the comparison in docs/access-models.md.',
 'RBAC is explained rather than falsely presented as a deployed role system.',
 {'53':'In DAC, the resource owner normally chooses discretionary grants, within administrator and mandatory-policy constraints.',
 '54':'An administrator or security policy authority defines MAC rules; ordinary file owners cannot bypass them merely by chmod.',
 '55':'Assign a database-administrator role the required database-management permissions, then grant/revoke role membership instead of duplicating per-user rights.',
 '56':'A Linux capability grants a narrower class of privileged kernel operations than unrestricted root; this is distinct from an object-capability design.',
 '57':'ACLs add named users/groups and a mask to the ordinary Unix mode design, permitting targeted exceptions. They increase review complexity and remain discretionary.'})
add(19,'Harden the Machine','Can three justified changes be verified and reversed?',
 'The audit will record services, ports, updates and firewall state. Incoming default-deny, service confinement and restrictive umask will be testable afterward.',
 'Hardening reduces unnecessary privilege and exposure while keeping a recovery path. apt update refreshes metadata; apt upgrade actually installs available updates.',
 'Permit management SSH before enabling UFW; run the teaching worker as nobody with no-new-privileges; set a 077 umask for future interactive lab files.',
 'Capture before/after, verify firewall default, process UID/NoNewPrivs and mode 0600 on a newly created file. Record package update output.',
 'UFW is not a complete Docker port policy: published Docker ports can bypass it. This project publishes no container ports. Kernel updates may require reboot and are reported separately.',
 {'58':'Keep only documented services needed by the lab: guest management, journald, policy enforcement and temporary mission services. Service necessity depends on the VM inventory.',
 '59':'Management SSH remains limited by the VM\'s host-loopback forwarding. The local SSH exercise binds only guest loopback. No container ports are published.',
 '60':'Patches remove known vulnerabilities in the trusted computing base and applications. Updating metadata without installing packages is not remediation.',
 '61':'A change can cause lockout or break a dependency; a snapshot, recorded previous configuration and a scoped rollback make recovery practical.'})
add(20,'Secure Remote Access','What does a key-authenticated localhost SSH session prove?',
 'A temporary key will authenticate to a guest-loopback-only sshd. The session will retain the student\'s non-root authorization.',
 'SSH authenticates the server host key and the client user\'s key possession. After login, Unix credentials and policy still determine access.',
 'Use pinned local host keys, key-only authentication, no root login and a loopback listener. Delete the temporary private key after use.',
 'Require LOCAL_SSH_SUCCESS, non-root id output, loopback bind and sshd effective settings; capture authentication journal.',
 'The isolated listener uses port 2222 so the managed Lima transport remains available. A localhost test does not prove a public network deployment is secure.',
 {'62':'The client verifies server host identity and the server verifies the user\'s credential (here an Ed25519 key). Encryption protects transport once negotiation succeeds.',
 '63':'The user\'s UID/GID, groups, DAC/ACLs, MAC and sudo policy determine allowed actions after authentication.',
 '64':'Use journalctl for the specific sshd unit; the accepted key and session events support login evidence. Other Ubuntu setups may also route logs to auth.log.'})
add(21,'Who Protects the VM?','Where do the VM trust boundaries end?',
 'The guest will identify ARM64 Ubuntu and virtualized hardware. The host remains macOS on Apple Silicon.',
 'A guest kernel enforces guest process boundaries, while virtual devices and Apple\'s virtualization/hypervisor stack implement the VM boundary. Lima manages lifecycle and SSH transport.',
 'Minimize shared interfaces; keep host mounts disabled, no bridged adapter, no published container ports and no unnecessary devices.',
 'Capture guest version/architecture and virtualization detection; document the actual stack rather than a generic x86 diagram.',
 'A guest compromise does not imply a host escape, but hypervisor/device bugs, shared folders and management channels add dependencies. No measured/secure boot or hardware TEE claim is made.',
 {'65':'Guest applications, privileged services and the guest kernel are inside the guest boundary; the hypervisor and host lie outside it but are part of its security dependencies.',
 '66':'The virtualization layer presents virtual hardware. In this setup Lima orchestrates a VM backed by Apple Virtualization.framework and the host hypervisor mechanisms.',
 '67':'The VM trusts virtual devices, hypervisor, host kernel and management software in addition to its guest TCB. Host compromise can undermine the guest.'})
add(22,'Practice: Secure the Machine','Can six scoped weaknesses be investigated, fixed and re-tested?',
 'The generated fixture will expose incorrect file/directory permissions, an unnecessary loopback listener, a root worker and missing integrity baselining. Each fix will have an original-condition retest.',
 'A baseline supports classification and evidence. Multiple OS controls address confidentiality, integrity, authorization, attack surface and privilege separation.',
 'Use restrictive modes/groups, stop the unwanted listener, run the worker as nobody with no-new-privileges and keep a root-protected integrity reference.',
 'Require all six F1-F6 VERIFIED markers, baseline/after inventories and successful authorized access after remediation.',
 'This is a practice assessment of intentionally seeded fixtures. The real instructor challenge, separate rules and image were not supplied, so it cannot be claimed completed.',
 {'68-75':'Baseline first; identify six findings; classify each; choose and apply a control; re-test the same condition; preserve logs and checksums; summarize evidence and limitations in the final report.',
 '76':'The most striking result is the AppArmor probe: both teaching files opened under DAC, yet the same program was denied one file after its profile was enforced. Compared with the temporary ACL grant for Bob, this shows that owner-controlled permission and mandatory application policy answer different authorization questions. The allowed-file control still succeeded, demonstrating selective confinement rather than a general application failure.',
 '77':'Protecting the incident record uses both directory traversal restrictions and file modes; integrity additionally needs a separately protected baseline. One control is insufficient.',
 '78':'The address-space maps and the stack-buffer-overflow diagnostic illustrate different boundaries: inter-process isolation versus memory safety inside one process.',
 '79':'Least privilege appears in group-based file access, named ACL grants, process capabilities, container restrictions, service identity and SSH authentication policy.',
 '80':'If the kernel is compromised, it can falsify logs, bypass permissions and read/alter process memory. Guest-level defenses no longer provide their assumed guarantees.',
 '81':'Protected resources include files, process memory, CPU/memory availability, credentials and communication endpoints.',
 '82':'Trusted components include the enforcing guest kernel, policy administrator, toolchain, baseline store and host virtualization stack.',
 '83':'Bob, malformed input, a compromised application and unintended clients represent distinct untrusted subjects in the scoped experiments.',
 '84':'Boundaries include user/kernel mode, per-process address spaces, identity-based file access, mandatory profiles, namespaces and the VM boundary.',
 '85':'The kernel enforces modes/ACLs, memory mappings, capabilities, namespaces and cgroups; AppArmor adds mandatory mediation.',
 '86':'Denial normally returns an error or terminates a violating process. We record exit status and diagnostics and may receive policy audit logs.',
 '87':'Misconfiguration, implementation bugs, excessive privilege or a compromised trusted layer can defeat a mechanism. A passing lab test covers only its stated condition.',
 '88':'Re-run the original negative test, retain positive-function tests, record actual output and exit codes, and verify evidence hashes against a trusted copy.'})
Path('docs/mission-notes.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows,indent=2)+'\n')
for r in rows:
 text=f'# Mission {r["id"]:02d}: {r["title"]}\n\n'
 for k in ['question','prediction','concept','defense','verification','reflection']:
  text+=f'## {k.title()}\n\n{r[k]}\n\n'
 text+='## Experiment\n\nRun `python3 scripts/run_lab.py --missions '+str(r['id'])+'` inside the prepared guest. See `missions/run.sh` for exact commands; missions 18 and 22 use the documented prerequisites.\n\n'
 text+='## Observation\n\nThe generated report links actual mission logs and reports their verification status. Predictions above are not measurements.\n\n## Handout questions\n\n'
 for q,answer in r['answers'].items(): text+=f'**Q{q}.** {answer}\n\n'
 Path(f'notes/mission-{r["id"]:02d}.md').write_text(text)
